    {
      "raw_rank": 8,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00121",
      "distance": 0.9142969846725464,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 205,
      "page_end": 205,
      "text": "This, however, is precisely what renders advertising, or at least much of it, shrill. Forms of rhetoric that disclose the world and place human beings as resources for maximizing advantage, as most advertising does, eschew or foreclose the rich ecological relationality of being-in-the-world. That is, such forms deattune us. One cannot simply castigate this move. of course. One ought not simply say that advertising is wrong or evil, nor am I making any such argument. But neither should we ignore how advertising can convey narrowly suasive aspects of our being to disproportionally outsize all others, like eating nothing but cake all day. Other ambient variables that could condition advertising in its specific messages (you need cheap tacos at 2 a.m. !) or larger sayings (Consume!) are dimmed. This dimming contributes simultaneously to advertising's power and to our phenomenological reaction to it as shrill, as too sweet. A diet of advertising is unsustainable. And for this reason, while advertising is rhetoric, neither good nor ill, it would be an error to posit it as illuminating rhetoric's basic principle. Of course, this is precisely what, say, philosophy commonly does: thinly slice rhetoric as a manipulative version of persuasion, so that we see its shrillest forms as constituting its primary essence. A \"sustainable\" rhetoric (I discuss sustainability and sufficiency in chapters 7 and 8) is one that brings us to the richer experiences already spoken through language's saying, not solely as a sign system, but as the ch orographic background resounding of our attunement to the world.in\n\nHeidegger's theory oflanguage allows us to rethink rhetoric as persuasion so as to take it out of a delimiting subject/object framework. Persuasion is certainly an affordance of rhetorical interaction, but an ambient perspective lets us see that taking rhetoric as persuasion narrowly and subjectively defined deflects our emplacement in the background \"relation of all relations\" that is language and distances us from the conditioning sway of the nonhuman that also gives place and bearing to rhetoric (Heidegger, OWL 135). Rhetoric emerges from our being-in-the-world, then, precisely also as a response to the way the world attunes and speaks us, but a response that allows for new forms of revealing that transform, at least potentially, how we are in relation to the world. This grants that even ifwe keep rhetoric always within language's orbit, language is never simply the doing of human beings, so that the wellspring of what rhetoric is and can be is opened through an attunement to its ambience. That is, rhetoric not only\n\nreceives its bearings from an attunement to all aspects of worldly existence that give rise to ways of being, human and nonhuman, but does so without mistaking any given presencing as ultimate being. Rhetoric thereby shares in a partiality that, rather than limits us, offers us hope for further and enriching unfoldings in rhetorical being.\n\nThe Rhetorical Thing\n\nObjective, Subjective, Ambient\n\nOnly when man becomes the subject do non-human beings become objects.\n\n-Martin Heidegger, Parmenides\n\n\"Words and things\" is the entirely serious title ofa problem. -Michel Foucault, The Archaeology of Knowledge\n\nObjects themselves, far from the insipid physical bulks that one imagines, are already aflame with ambiguity, torn by vibrations and insurgencies equaling those found in the mosttortured human moods.\n\n-Graham Harman, Tool-Being\n\nRhetoric has always dealt with things, which is to say that rhetoric has not ignored the material realm. The field's historically predominant focus on rhetor, audience, and language may obscure this point, but contemporary rhetorical theory in particular attends to materiality. For instance, the field has firmly incorporated Marx's notion of dialectical materialism and its attendant critique of ideology. In addition, substantial contemporary work has investigated technology, institutions, and bodies. Indeed, even the notion of materiality is increasingly thematized in its own right as worthy of direct scholarly attention (Biesecker and Lucaites, Kochin). But the problem has always been how and to what extent materiality itself matters. As a long tradition has had it, rhetoric, being one of the seven liberal arts and crucial for the formation of what we call the humanities, is most fundamentally an affair of human beings and their dealings. It is overwhelmingly discursive, a verbal art. So, yes, things matter, but as objects of concern for rhetoric or as part of the",
